  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThis afternoon, rain showers will continue to spread across the\nNorthland as a surface low cuts across Wisconsin into the Great\nLakes. This main band of rain currently moving through far \nnortheastern Minnesota and northwest Wisconsin will continue to \nmove east throughout this evening and overnight, with the \nheaviest rainfall falling across northwest Wisconsin. Models are\nstill having trouble with a cold bias overnight, so brought up \nminimum temperatures for tonight due to cloud cover, as done in \nprevious forecasts.\n\nWrap around showers will develop and more off-and-on rain \nshowers are expected across much of the area in the late night \nand early morning Friday. Lake enhanced/effect rain showers \nwill continue through most of Friday morning across portions of\nthe South Shore as cold air moves over the relatively warm \nwaters of Lake Superior. A few snowflakes could mix in across \nfar northern Minnesota during the overnight hours, but no\naccumulations and no impacts are expected. Strong north winds \nwill surge behind the departing boundary, gusting between 25 and\n35 mph inland and up to 45 mph near Lake Superior, before \nconditions subside and skies clear late Friday afternoon.\n\nHigh pressure will build across the Upper Midwest Friday night\ninto Saturday, bringing a return of sunny skies and dry air.\nRHs increased significantly Saturday with the initial population\nof forecast data, so decreased them again to better account for\ndry air aloft that will likely mix down to the surface that day.\nNo fire headlines are anticipated at this time, but fire weather\nconditions will still be near critical with the combined lower \nRH of 25 to 30 percent and gusty southwest winds of 20-30 mph \nexpected that day. Under this system, temperatures will increase\nback toward sixty degrees through the weekend into next week. \n\nOn Sunday, an upper-level shortwave will pass north of the\ninternational border, dragging a mostly dry cold front through\nour region with only scattered passing clouds. Winds will\nfollow as this system passes with gusts 20-30 mph in northwest\nWisconsin. RH values between 30 and 40% will lead to elevated \nfire weather conditions with higher winds, but not anticipating\nany fire headlines needed. Behind this boundary, upper-level \nridging will become the dominant pattern driver across the Great\nLakes. This synoptic regime will maintain dry, mostly clear, \nand unseasonably mild weather across our area through Monday, \nwith afternoon temperatures warming well into the sixties.\n\nDry and quiet weather will persist into Tuesday as the upper \nridge remains centered over the region, keeping temperatures \nmild. By Wednesday, a weak passing upper disturbance will bring \nan increase in cloud cover along with a slight chance of \nafternoon rain showers. However, any precipitation will be light\nand widely scattered, with dry conditions still favored overall\nat this point.",
